        {/* ── Section: Making Tax Digital ── */}
        <AccordionSection id="making-tax-digital" title="Making Tax Digital for Income Tax">
          <p class="mb-3">
            Making Tax Digital (MTD) for Income Tax is HMRC's programme to digitise tax reporting. It's being introduced in phases based on your qualifying income (gross self-employment income plus property income combined):
          </p>
          <ul class="list-none space-y-3 mb-4">
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="shrink-0 font-semibold text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400">From 6 Apr 2026</span>
              <span>Qualifying income over <strong>£50,000</strong> (based on 2024/25 return)</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="shrink-0 font-semibold text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400">From 6 Apr 2027</span>
              <span>Qualifying income over <strong>£30,000</strong> (based on 2025/26 return)</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="shrink-0 font-semibold text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400">From 6 Apr 2028</span>
              <span>Qualifying income over <strong>£20,000</strong> (based on 2026/27 return)</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="shrink-0 font-semibold text-[var(--text-muted)]">Not yet confirmed</span>
              <span>Qualifying income <strong>£20,000 or under</strong> — currently not in scope</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mb-3 text-sm">
            Source: <a href="https://www.gov.uk/guidance/check-if-youre-eligible-for-making-tax-digital-for-income-tax" class="text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GOV.UK — Check if you're eligible for Making Tax Digital for Income Tax</a>
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mt-4 mb-2" style="color: var(--text-primary)">What MTD requires</h3>
          <p class="mb-3">
            If you're in scope, you must use <strong>MTD-compatible software</strong> to keep digital records and submit <strong>quarterly updates</strong> to HMRC (instead of just one annual return). Popular MTD-compatible options include FreeAgent, Xero, QuickBooks, and Sage.
          </p>

          <CalloutBox variant="warning" title="Important: spreadsheets alone are NOT MTD-compliant">
            A plain spreadsheet (Google Sheets or Excel) does not meet MTD requirements on its own. You would need approved <strong>bridging software</strong> to link your spreadsheet to HMRC's MTD system. If you're in scope for MTD, the simplest option is to switch to MTD-compatible accounting software.
          </CalloutBox>

          <p class="mt-3">
            If you're not yet in scope for MTD, a spreadsheet or manual tracker remains perfectly acceptable for keeping records and filing your annual Self Assessment return.
          </p>
        </AccordionSection>

        {/* ── Section: Spreadsheet vs Ready-Made ── */}
        <AccordionSection id="spreadsheet-vs-tracker" title="Build Your Own Spreadsheet or Use a Ready-Made Tracker?">
          <p class="mb-3">
            You have two main options for tracking your freelance finances without full accounting software:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mt-4 mb-2" style="color: var(--text-primary)">Option 1: Build your own spreadsheet</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 mb-3">
            <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Free, fully customisable, you understand every formula</li>
            <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Takes time to set up properly, easy to miss categories, formulas can break</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mb-3">
            If you go this route, create separate tabs for income and expenses. Use the HMRC categories listed above so your data maps directly to the Self Assessment form. Add a summary tab that auto-calculates totals by category and month.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mt-4 mb-2" style="color: var(--text-primary)">Option 2: Use a ready-made tracker</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 mb-3">
            <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Pre-built with HMRC categories, formulas already working, saves hours of setup</li>
            <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Small upfront cost, less customisable than a blank sheet</li>
          </ul>

          <CalloutBox variant="tip" title="The key thing">
            Whether you build or buy, consistency matters more than the tool. The best tracker is the one you actually use every week. Pick one approach and stick with it — don't let receipts pile up for months.
          </CalloutBox>
        </AccordionSection>

